17] [17592186050613 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050613 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050613 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050613 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving One-Step Multiplication and Division Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050613 #edb/ref 1033 40 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050613 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "58d4d7b7-4b91-5e3c-ba04-7522d5cfa8f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186208826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186208827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186208828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186208829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1031 "Ratios" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1033 6056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050709 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "607ed358-6a5e-5a58-b89a-0334343a1121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151824 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13098" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151824 #edb/ref 1071 "Adam started writing a book. There is a proportional relationship between the number of pages $x$ he writes and the book's thickness $y$ (in millimeters). Adam measured the thickness of his book to be $3$ millimeters after he'd written $18$ pages.\n\nWhat is the constant of proportionality for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151824 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e85107d6-ecdf-526d-a7ce-091d028039eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151824 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that after Adam wrote $x=18$ pages, the book's thickness was $y=3$ millimeters.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{3}{18} = \\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{18 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $\\dfrac{1}{6},$ and its units are \"millimeters per page.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151825 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13099" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151825 #edb/ref 1071 "Beatrice needs to clean the garage. So, to motivate herself, she allows herself $10$ minutes of video game time for every $25$ minutes she spends cleaning.\n\nThere is a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in minutes) Beatrice spends cleaning the garage and the time $y$ (in minutes) she plays video games. What equation represents the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151825 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15f20ecd-503f-5748-b004-680b5fc127c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151825 #edb/ref 1074 "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that for every $x=25$ minutes Beatrice spends cleaning, she will play $y=10$ minutes of video games.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{10}{25} = \\dfrac{10 \\div 5}{25 \\div 5} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{2}{5} x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151826 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151826 #edb/ref 1071 "Amy is baking a loaf of bread. If the loaf weighs $12$ ounces, then Amy needs to bake the loaf for $40$ minutes. There is a proportional relationship between the loaf's weight (in ounces) and the time (in minutes) the loaf needs to bake.\n\nIf Amy leaves the house for $60$ minutes and wants the loaf to be fully baked the moment she returns, how much should the loaf of bread weigh?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151826 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b17572d-a8f7-523f-9697-ae16f3d84398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151826 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the loaf's weight (in ounces), and $y$ be the time (in minutes) the loaf needs to bake.\n\nFirst, we need to construct the equation that represents the proportional relationship. Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that if the loaf weighs $x=12$ ounces, then Amy needs to bake it for $y=40$ minutes. We can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{40}{12} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 4}{12 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{10}{3} x.\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the value of $x$ so that the loaf will bake for exactly $y=60$ minutes. So, we substitute $y=60$ into the equation above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =\\frac{10}{3}x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{10}{3},$ which is $\\dfrac{3}{10}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}\\times \\frac{10}{3}x & =\\frac{3}{10}\\times 60 \\\\ 1x & =\\frac{3\\times 60}{10} \\\\ x & =\\frac{180}{10} \\\\ x & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the loaf of bread should weigh $18$ ounces." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152270 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152270 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152270 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152270 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152270 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152270 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186498574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152270 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Constant of Proportionality" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152270 #edb/ref 104