/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498453 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498453 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c2bad046-a923-58f2-bdea-1c860f46c975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498454 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498454 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498454 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498454 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c5f44313-2822-5c69-bc24-8d6493b66567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498455 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498455 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498455 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498455 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498455 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498455 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498455 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498455 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186497845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498455 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c7a06b64-073c-57ea-a18a-32470f23b71e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498458 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498458 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498458 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498458 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498458 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498458 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498458 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498458 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186497861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498458 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cb2de326-5c77-59a4-8469-5f6eb55c27a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498476 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498476 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498476 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498476 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186497881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498476 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e95dfe78-b1e7-5a61-bce1-8aaf37b7c796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498489 #edb/ref 1069 "q-92002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498489 #edb/ref 1071 "William read $72$ pages of a best-selling novel in $3$ hours. There is a proportional relationship between time $x$ William spends reading (in hours) and the number of pages $y$ he reads. What is the equation that represents the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "094ce64c-3d3e-57b3-9487-feae2ee86b9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498489 #edb/ref 1074 "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that William read $y=72$ pages of the novel in $x=3$ hours.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{72}{3}=24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=24x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498523 #edb/ref 1069 "q-185468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498523 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498523 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $p$ is directly proportional to $q.$ Given that $p=-35$ when $q=-7$, find the value of $p$ when $q=-6.$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498523 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3fee2173-5ead-559d-b784-c9f6f5226ecd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498523 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $p$ varies directly with $q,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\np=kq.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $p=-35$ when $q=-7.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-35 & =k\\cdot (-7) \\\\ \\frac{(-35)}{(-7)} & =k \\\\ \\frac{35}{7} & =k \\\\ 5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\np=5q.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $q=-6$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =5\\cdot (-6)=-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498525 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498525 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498525 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498525 #edb/ref 1071 "Andry is planning a party. He made $30$ sandwiches for $12$ people at the last party. If there is a proportional relationship between the number of people at the party $x$ and the number of sandwiches $y,$ what is the constant of proportionality for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498525 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41a80b0b-ddbc-5443-a94b-c85298f9a320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498525 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that Andry made $y=30$ sandwiches for $x=12$ people.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{30}{12}=\\frac{30÷6}{12÷6}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $\\frac{5}{2},$ and its units are \"sandwiches per person.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498527 #edb/ref 1069 "q-91985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498527 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498527 #edb/ref 1071 "Tom purchased $7$ gallons of gas for $$21$ dollars. If there is a proportional relationship between the cost of gas $y$ (in dollars) and the volume of gas $x$ (in gallons), what is the constant of proportionality for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498527 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48d16888-fa09-5ea2-9260-b0fddb4cefed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498527 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that Tom paid $y=21$ dollars for $x=7$ gallons of gas.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{21}{7}=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $3$, and its units are \"dollars per gallon.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498528 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498528 #edb/ref 1071 "Jane is making lemonade for her friends. According to the recipe, $4$ cups of lemon juice are required for $18$ glasses of lemonade. There is a proportional relationship between the number of cups of lemon juice and the number of glasses of lemonade. If Jane needs to make $12$ glasses of lemonade, how many cups of lemon juice does she need?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48f6d6fb-eebd-57a1-90a2-1f718d690dc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498528 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the number of cups of lemon juice and $y$ be the number of glasses of lemonade.\n\nFirst, we need to construct the equation that represents the proportional relationship. Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that $x=4$ cups of lemon juice are required for $y=18$ glasses of lemonade. We can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{18}{4}=\\frac{18÷2}{4÷2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{9}{2}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the value of $x$ so that Jane can make $y=12$ glasses of lemonade. So, we substitute $y=12$ into the equation above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =\\frac{9}{2}x \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of $\\frac{9}{2},$ which is $\\frac{2}{9}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}\\times \\frac{9}{2}x & =\\frac{2}{9}\\times 12 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\times 12}{9} \\\\ x & =\\frac{24}{9} \\\\ x & =\\frac{24÷3}{9÷3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}=2\\,\\text{R}2=2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jane requires $2\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ cups of lemon juice to make $12$ glasses of lemonade." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498574 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498574 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498574 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498574 #edb/ref 1071 "Caroline bought a new car. The company claims she can drive the vehicle for $120$ miles with $4$ gallons of gasoline. If there is a proportional relationship between the volume of gas $x$ (in gallons) and the distance traveled $y$ (in miles), what is the constant of proportionality for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498574 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9b1a1f6-9b02-546d-b293-0d13315885e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498574 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that Caroline can drive the car for $y=120$ miles with $x=4$ gallons of gasoline.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{120}{4}=\\frac{120÷4}{4÷4}=\\frac{30}{1}=30\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $30$, and its units are \"miles per gallon.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498600 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498600 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498600 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498600 #edb/ref 1071 "In a direct variation, $f=84$ when $g=-7.$ What is the relationship between $f$ and $g?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498600 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9cbb3a3-7c34-5581-b4b4-9fd9c86b9859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498600 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f$ varies directly with $g,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nf=kg.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that $f=84$ when $g=-7$ to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}84 & =k\\cdot (-7) \\\\ \\frac{84}{(-7)} & =k \\\\ -\\frac{84}{7} & =k \\\\ -12 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nf=-12g.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
